**Solution Explanation**

Let the grid have \(N = n \times m\) seats.  
Tina first paints exactly \(k\) seats. Then Rahul chooses an unpainted seat \(R\). Finally Tina chooses any seat (painted or not) other than \(R\).

Rahul wants to minimise the distance to Tina; Tina wants to maximise it. Both play optimally.

For a fixed painted set \(S\) of size \(k\), Rahul will pick an unpainted seat \(R\) that makes the distance as small as possible. Since Tina can later sit on any seat except \(R\) (including the painted ones), the distance after Rahul’s choice is  

\[
\max_{T \neq R} \operatorname{dist}(R,T).
\]

This is exactly the **eccentricity** of \(R\) in the whole grid – the farthest Manhattan distance from \(R\) to any other cell.

Thus Rahul chooses among the unpainted seats the one with the smallest eccentricity. Tina, who wants the final distance to be large, will therefore paint the \(k\) seats with the **smallest** eccentricities, forcing Rahul to take the seat with the \((k+1)\)-st smallest eccentricity.

Hence for each \(k\) the answer is:

\[
\text{ans}[k] = \text{the }(k+1)^{\text{st}}\text{ smallest eccentricity among all }N\text{ seats.}
\]

The eccentricity of a seat \((i,j)\) (using 0‑based indices) is

\[
e(i,j) = \max\bigl(
i+j,\; i+(m-1-j),\; (n-1-i)+j,\; (n-1-i)+(m-1-j)
\bigr).
\]

We compute \(e(i,j)\) for every cell, sort the list, and output it.

---

**Complexity**  

- Computing all eccentricities: \(O(N)\).  
- Sorting: \(O(N\log N)\).  
Total over all test cases: \(O(\sum N\log N) \le 10^5\log 10^5\).

